  "query_preview": "(Today through Sunday)...\nIssued at 316 AM EDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nEarly today through Wednesday...\n\nCentral Indiana will continue to rest amid the northeastern edge of \na predominant subtropical ridge occupying most of southern North \nAmerica...with seasonably modest northwest flow aloft and associated \nsmall/weak forcings...maintaining chances of occasional convective \nclusters.  Additionally, a weaker yet broad trough of surface low \npressure aligned from the central Plains across the northern \nMidwest, will promote moderate southwesterly breezes...bringing very \nwarm to hot conditions and increasingly oppressive humidity into the \nmid-week.\n\nThe resultant higher theta-E will contribute to instability \ngradient, although this will likely be strongest along the local \nregion's far northern/NE zones...if not even closer to the southern \nGreat Lakes.\nTherefore convective chances within the CWA will be greatest \nfollowing upstream development (northern IL) that then trends/curls \nSE-ward.\nBest chances for organized/severe storms will most likely be Tuesday \nPM-overnight hours, and again late Wednesday...with general focus I-\n74 corridor and northeast, with lowest confidence on severe \npotential over southwest counties.\n\nThat said, cannot rule out isolated RW-s at overall less favorable \ntimes given the warm/humid boundary layer...including isolated \nthunder later today north of a Lafayette to New Castle line, and \nacross much of the CWA tonight.  Expect temperatures through \nWednesday to range from 68-75F to 85-93F, with a distinct NNE-SSW \ngradient, which may be increased by greater cloud cover over NE \ncounties Tue-Wed.  \n\nThursday through Monday...\n\nRemainder of the period to most likely feature see-saw of conditions \nas quasi-stationary surface cool front along the northern side of \nvery broad and very humid subtropical ridge...probably slides south \nby late Thursday, placing the region in milder easterly flow...with \nthe boundary expected to then push north, returning humid southerly \nflow for the weekend.  Most periods to yield at least low POPs per \nthe corresponding frontal zone never really pushing south of the \nCWA, although overall convective chances after Thursday night should \nbe much lower than the early/mid-week.  Readings to range from \nmainly 60s to 80s.",
